   "note": "delta_gdp follows the OBR's published multiplier convention, not model dynamics: impact multiplier 0.6 for current (day-to-day) departmental spending (OBR, Economic and fiscal outlook, July 2015, Box 3.2 'Fiscal multipliers' (obr.uk/box/fiscal-multipliers); taper restated in OBR, 'Dynamic scoring of policy measures in OBR forecasts' (articles.obr.uk)), fading linearly to zero over 20 quarters from the shock start. The linear path shape is this repo's choice \u2014 the OBR publishes the impact value and the zero endpoint, not the path. Imposed as a GDPM add-factor on the shocked run only; the model's own channel for this instrument is an accounting identity (1.0-flat passthrough) and cannot produce this profile. Component deltas (delta_cons_m, delta_if_m) are NOT overridden \u2014 they are what the model's own equations give in the imposed run, so e.g. delta_if_m stays exactly zero for a capital-spending shock even though delta_gdp follows the convention. Under the nominal-GDP pin (l.690) delta_cons_m is the income channel's response to the imposed GDP path (nominal GDP -> profits -> household income -> consumption), a few per cent of the GDP delta; the add-factor absorbs it, so the GDP total is still exactly the convention.",
